## CRITICAL FIX: bootstrap-cascade-status defect (MAJ-CORR-002 + MAJ-SAFE-003 + MAJ-CG-001)

**Bug:** the new `.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml` (introduced in fix-round-6) triggers on `paths: ['docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/**', ...]`. The 024B PR includes `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B/DISPATCH-NOTE.md` in its diff. The workflow runs the script, which finds MULTIPLE `cascade-status:` references in 024B's DISPATCH-NOTE (worked-example matrix `cascade-status: onboarded`, `cascade-status: onboarded-operator-bump`, `cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict` + the slice's own first-line `cascade-status: not applicable` declaration). Per fix-round-2's MAJ-BYPASS-002 fix the script asserts exactly-one match; with N>1 matches the script exits 1, causing the 024B PR's own CI to red.

This is a meta-bootstrap problem: the workflow wires up enforcement for cohort cascade slices (024C-F) but the slice that introduces the workflow (024B) is itself NOT a cohort cascade slice and shouldn't be subject to the check.

**Fix (preferred — path-filter approach):** in `.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml`, change the path filter so 024B's own dir is excluded:
```yaml
on:
  pull_request:
    branches: [main]
    types: [opened, synchronize, reopened]
    paths:
      - 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[C-Z]*/**'
      - 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[a-z]*/**'  # case variations defensive
      - 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md'
      - 'STATUS.md'
    paths-ignore:
      - 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B/**'
      - 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024A/**'
```
Note: `paths-ignore` is mutex-evaluated with `paths`. The intent is to allow cohort cascade slice DISPATCH-NOTE changes (024C, 024D, 024E, 024F, 024G) to fire the check while explicitly excluding 024A (plan-doc, no DISPATCH-NOTE) and 024B (this slice, tooling). Use a glob that captures only 024C-024Z for forward compatibility.

Add a comment block at the top of the workflow file explaining the path-filter rationale (cite this fix-round + the bootstrap defect).

**Also fix the script's behaviour for non-cascade DISPATCH-NOTEs (defensive):** in `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh`, add a pre-check: if the DISPATCH-NOTE's `cascade-status:` value is exactly `not applicable` (after stripping leading/trailing whitespace), exit 0 with a stdout message `OK: DISPATCH-NOTE declares cascade-status: not applicable; not a cohort cascade slice — nothing to enforce`. This makes the script self-aware and prevents accidental fail-closed on non-cascade slices that happen to be in scope (e.g., tooling slices, plan-doc updates). The `not applicable` value is added to the script's allowed-set as a fourth recognized value (alongside the 3 cascade-status enum values), but with semantics 'no enforcement runs'. Update the fail-closed default error message to enumerate all 4 recognized values.

Update plan-doc references: amend `docs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md` §2 invariant 2 to acknowledge the `not applicable` carve-out for tooling slices (024A, 024B, 024G) — these are NOT cohort cascade slices and don't onboard adopters; their DISPATCH-NOTEs declare `cascade-status: not applicable` to opt out of the CI check. The 3-element enum {onboarded, onboarded-operator-bump, blocked-on-adopter-conflict} continues to apply to cohort cascade slices (024C-F).

